A||N||16||Monkeyman||WTF--My battery's dead!||||8||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||05-01-2000||11:10 AM||||Can a battery die just sitting in a car?  I bought a new Sears Diehard battery for the Grape Ape (still not running) about 3 weeks ago.  I could operate the headlights, door locks, and windows just fine with it.  I haven't done anything to the car (no stereo yet, etc).  I just went out to install my new steering wheel and realized that my battery is dead!  There isn't even enough juice to pop the trunk.  (Good thing I replaced the lock cylinder!)  I was messing with the car the other day and I still had juice left.  What could have gone wrong?  Help!!!!!!!!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Old Lar||05-01-2000||04:14 PM||||Something is shorting out. Or if you are like me you turned the interior reading lights on and forgot to turn it off.  Killed the battery.  Or the trunk lid was ajar and the trunk light was on for a couple of days ..killed the battery.<P>Charge the battery to run an errand, dead battery when finished.  How humiliating to be jump started by a fork lift.||||reg||3
Z||000002||fierospeeder||05-01-2000||04:19 PM||||My fiero batteries slowly discharges after a few weeks so i usually just unplug one of the cables.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DJRice||05-01-2000||04:36 PM||||Monkey, <BR>When you get the battery recharged, heres a quick test to see if something is shorting out.  <P>Pull each fuse out one by one.  If you see sparks jump for any fuses other than the Radio fuse (because radio memory will be drawing power) or the courtesy lights (Because dome lights will be on with the door open) you have a short somewhere in that circuit.<P>There probably isnt much wrong, but if you were tinkering in the car the other day and spent hours with the doors open, and playing with headlights or whatever, you could have run the battery way down.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||triker||05-01-2000||05:02 PM||||I've let my 87GT sit for up to four months during the winter and have it start right up in the spring.  I always expect it to be dead but never is.  I know it's not good to let them sit, but there are too many cinders flying and vehicles sliding around here in the winter to take a chance on driving it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||05-01-2000||05:10 PM||||Thanx, guys.  I had the door open for a while the other day, but I removed the dome lights (going to put a radio in it's place) and didn't have the trunk open.  I can't think of anything else that would have caused a drain.  I rolled the window down and back up and used the power door locks once (4 or 5 taps on the button to get it to completely lock or unlock!), but I didn't do anything else.  I guess it's time to call Sears and get a new replacement battery.  Then I'll try the fuse check thing.  Wasn't there some other way of checking to see if there's a drain by using a multimeter??||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||05-01-2000||06:03 PM||||My battery is always completely drained after 4 days.<P>Triker: that's really, really weird that your battery isn't drained after 4 months. I had calculated once that the ECM will drain a 50AH battery in 15 days or so.||||reg||1
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||05-01-2000||08:00 PM||||I had a brand new battery discharge totally by letting it sit on concrete one time, but charged it back up and it was fine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||olympic||05-02-2000||03:17 AM||||   Wouldn't a multimeter tell you the current being drawn from your battery simply by placing the probes on the battery terminals???||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||05-02-2000||11:24 AM||||I hope to get the Grape Ape running in the next few weeks, so I'll see if it keeps happening when I drive it every day.  (12 hours at work, 8 hours to drive <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">, 3 hours to sleep and a quick shower!!!)  Cliff, you've given me hope that something else isn't wrong with the car.  BTW-What kind of battery do you have (50AH)???  Mine has 820CCA.  Is this the same?  Now I need a new battery charger.  I'd let the shop (that's doing the motor swap) charge it up, but they'd probably charge me $100 for it!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Walter A||05-03-2000||12:17 AM||||After having received good info from this Forum for 1yr I may finally have something to contribute.  Have had similar problem(s).<BR>First occasion it was the headlight relay on passanger side. Went through all the suggested traces and found nothing. If you have no multi-meter simply disconnect your<BR>ground terminal from a charged battery and<BR>touch it to the post and watch for a spark indicating a current draw.(first disconnect<BR>engine compartment light) I found the problem<BR>the fist time by accident whikle pulling the<BR>fuses. Kept hearing a relay click. Installed new relay but do not believe I had to. Ended up having to clean terminals on plug to<BR>solve problem.  Next time around went through all of the above with no joy. To make<BR>a long story short it was the oil pressure sending unit. Replaced and have not had a<BR>problem for past 8 mos. Good Luck..||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fret Naught||05-03-2000||01:03 AM||||Here is how you can test your current draw with a multi meter. 1.The meter must have the capability to test current 2. The leads must be plugged into the correct holes on your meter one will be common, and the other will be amp or ma. 3. disconnect the positive terminal on your battery and hook the meter up in series with it. Red lead on battery post, black lead on battery cable.<BR>Remember, most multilmeters are only good for a couple of amps with out a shunt. But if you are just looking for a trickle discharge that is how you do it. A quick way I test it is to lift one of the leads on your battery, then touch it back on the post any load will cause a slight spark. Safety wise that is probably not the smartest thing to do, but as long as there is no fuel odor or hydrogen gas build up (smell it to) I will usually do it.||||reg||12
Z||000012||DJRice||05-03-2000||09:10 AM||||I guess its a good thing that most of the Fieros on the road aren't fueled by Hydrogen....<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Fret Naught||05-03-2000||09:43 AM||||Yup, good thing, unfortunately, hydrogen is a by product of batteries when they discharge. Not a real problem in the Fiero, it is well vented. But under a hood in a closed area BOOM!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000014||prof bobo||05-03-2000||11:14 AM||||If you don't have a meter, or don't have one  capable of reading 1's or 10's of amps, just place a small 12v light in series with your battery.<P>Disconnect negative lead.  Connect light (12v, low current bulb) between negative battery post and negative lead.  The light should come on.  You can't draw more current through a lightbulb than it normally conducts anyway (unless you raise the voltage) so you can even do this to _safely_ find direct short circuits.  Start disconnecting all of the things you _know_ draw power (ECM, radio, dome lights, etc).  If the light remains on, you got a problem.  Start pulling fuses one by one until the light goes out and  then you've isolated the faulty circuit.  Examine everything on that circuit and eventually you'll find the problem.  Now go back and repeat the process to ensure that there was only one problem.<P>Hint:  I've taken some old blown fuses and attached lights across the terminals so I can debug circuits on an individual basis.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Monkeyman||05-03-2000||12:30 PM||||I have a multimeter (cheap one, but it should work).  Should I have any drain with the key off and nothing on?  (No radio or dome lights to worry about.)  What should the meter read with the ignition off?  No sound from the headlight motors, so they aren't running on.  I can't think of anything that could be causing a drain.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Fret Naught||05-03-2000||01:06 PM||||With the key off and the trunk light pulled, unless the ecm draw current at all times I would so no you should see no current flow. I am not experienced enough with the Fiero yet to say for sure. Need to hear from Orief or one of the other Yoda's on line.<BR>Good luck||||reg||1
